<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 05: Center of Mass</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 4.1 through 4.3
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability and upload your work to the "Quiz 5" assignment in Canvas.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
            Make a drawing of three particles interacting via any internal forces you want, as well as some external
            forces. Draw the FBD for the three particles and label the forces that cancel when you follow the procedure
            leading to Equation 4.1 in the text.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	 Look for a stroboscopic picture online (attach it to your work) that shows the parabolic motion of the 
             center of mass under the action of gravity while an object is flying through the air. Do you see any
             features of the motion of the object that are not captured by the motion of the center of mass?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          		Example 4.2 <q>leaves it as a problem</q> for you to show something. Prove that statement here.
                You can do this using vectors in full generality, or being clever with your choice of coordinate system.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                In example 4.3, the total mass of the stick is <m>\frac{\lambda_0 L}{2}</m>. What fraction of the
                total mass is to the left of the center of mass? Does this point to a danger in the term 
                <q>center of mass</q>? 
            </p>
        </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>